{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"The file /Users/evintunador/.claude/jobs/8f1a9339/tmp/prbody.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)","tool_use_id":"toolu_01ASwGRfHnnRZzcLP7htUgGz","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"worktree-more-cli-adapters","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/.claude/worktrees/more-cli-adapters","head":"4fed9c92c790d651bc671f09ab8d881f1641ab11","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"id":"ev2-8b24c50eb5519bb559095aaeb2e0778c2e292bba81cc353305ce51001994da7a","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"session_id":"d10310cd-8144-4046-87b9-97bbb6ace18c","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.227"},"occurred_at":"2026-08-11T10:58:30.919Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/.claude/worktrees/more-cli-adapters","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"worktree-more-cli-adapters","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"The file /Users/evintunador/.claude/jobs/8f1a9339/tmp/prbody.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)","tool_use_id":"toolu_01ASwGRfHnnRZzcLP7htUgGz","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"7cd1021c-762b-442b-af4c-bda7c9106276","promptId":"835fd367-6328-4760-93f8-175628bea5c6","sessionId":"d10310cd-8144-4046-87b9-97bbb6ace18c","sessionKind":"bg","session_id":"d10310cd-8144-4046-87b9-97bbb6ace18c","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7cd1021c-762b-442b-af4c-bda7c9106276","timestamp":"2026-08-11T10:58:30.919Z","toolUseResult":{"content":"Adds capture for the two CLIs at the top of the README's TODO adapter list. **Rebased onto 0.19.0** and rewritten to its bar — no skip lists, every record gets a kind, sub-conversations linked.\n\nEvery format claim below comes from a live transcript or from the CLI's own shipped source, never inference. Both adapters have since been driven by hand against live Gemini CLI 0.54.0 and Qwen Code 0.21.5, which is where most of the interesting content of this PR came from — see [Verification](#verification).\n\n## Gemini CLI\n\n`~/.gemini/tmp/<project>/chats/session-*.jsonl`, hooked on `AfterAgent` + `SessionEnd`.\n\nThe file is append-only but is **not** a transcript — it is a write-ahead log of mutations to one conversation document: `$rewindTo` removals, `$set` metadata patches, whole-document `$set.messages` snapshots, and message upserts keyed by a durable id. Capture replays it with Gemini's own record precedence, verified line-by-line against `loadConversationRecord` in the shipped bundle.\n\n**It deliberately does not honour the removals.** On a real session, Gemini rewrote the document with a snapshot that silently dropped the prompt the user had just typed, so a faithful replay records no trace of it having been asked. The ledger keeps the union of every message the file ever held, each at its last-written value.\n\nEach removal is also recorded **in its own right**, as an `activity` naming the ids it withdrew — `rewind` for an explicit `$rewindTo`, `snapshot_drop` for a snapshot that quietly omits what the document used to hold. Both paths matter, and the snapshot is the one the real incident took.\n\nEverything that is not a turn is recorded too: `$set` patches become `session_state`, and `info`/`error`/`warning` notices become turns spoken by the system (Gemini discards them; \"quota exceeded\" is often the only explanation for why a turn looks abandoned). Sub-sessions under `<chats>/<parent session id>/` become sub-conversations.\n\nBecause nothing is ever removed, `seq` is assigned at first sighting — over messages and mutation records alike, so they share one number space — and never reused. A rewind therefore cannot renumber later turns; the positional-`seq` churn the opencode adapter accepts does not arise here.\n\n## Qwen Code\n\n`~/.qwen/projects/<escaped-cwd>/chats/<session-id>.jsonl`, hooked on `Stop` + `SessionEnd`.\n\nA fork of Gemini CLI that did *not* inherit its conversation store: plain append-only JSONL with a claude-code-shaped envelope, so this adapter is the claude-code adapter's twin — per-line conversion, line-index `seq`, a torn-tail-safe cursor, the same catch-up sweep.\n\nEverything that is not a turn arrives as a `system` line discriminated by `subtype`:\n\n| subtype | kind |\n|---|---|\n| `attribution_snapshot` | `file_snapshot` — per-file hashes are already **in** the record, so nothing needs resolving against a machine-local cache and `resolved` stays empty |\n| `custom_title`, `session_source`, `parent_session`, `goal_state` | `session_state` |\n| `at_command`, `agent_bootstrap`, `agent_launch_prompt` | `context_injection` |\n| `slash_command` | `activity`, **attributed to the human** — it carries the literal keystrokes, and Qwen marks it `sentToModel: false`, so it is not a turn the model saw but it is very much a person's action |\n| everything else, including subtypes a future Qwen adds | `activity` |\n\nThe type list is Qwen's own `RECORD_TYPES`, read out of its shipped sources, so only a genuinely new top-level type routes to the drift path.\n\nBoth CLIs express content as Google GenAI `Part` lists, so that conversion lives once in `adapters/genai-parts.ts`.\n\n## Shared cursor, and a bug fix for codex\n\n0.19.0 taught the claude-code cursor three things the other adapters still did not know: **absent is not zero**, **`size` bounds the sweep**, and **the write must be atomic**. Each was worth having everywhere rather than in one of five copies, so the cursor moved into `adapters/common.ts` and all five adapters now use it — field names preserved per adapter, so no cursor on disk resets.\n\nThat also surfaced that **codex still had the torn-trailing-line bug** 0.19.0 fixed for claude-code: the cursor advanced past a half-written line, so the completed line was never captured. Fixed with the same narrow rule (unterminated file *and* final line).\n\n## The hook timeout, which is the bug worth reading about\n\n`cledger install` wrote `timeout: 120` into all three Claude-shaped configs. **Claude Code reads that field as seconds. Gemini CLI and Qwen Code copied Claude's hook config format but not its unit** — both pass the number straight to `setTimeout` (`Hook timed out after ${timeout}ms`, `DEFAULT_HOOK_TIMEOUT = 6e4`).\n\nSo on those two, every hook was SIGTERMed after **120 milliseconds**. A cold capture is ~85ms of work on top of node's startup, so it lost that race nearly every time — and whether `appendEvents` finished before the signal decided whether the events survived. The symptom was Gemini printing `Hook(s) [...] failed` on almost every turn while its events *sometimes* still landed.\n\nDiagnosed by trapping the signal in a wrapper script driven by a real session: the hook logged `caught SIGTERM` in the same second it started.\n\n`install` now writes each CLI's own unit, and **repairs an existing hook's timeout in place** — it previously saw \"a cledger hook is already here\" and returned, so nobody already installed could have fixed this by re-running it. `src/test/install.test.ts` is the first test this module has had, which is how a unit mismatch in a hardcoded literal survived.\n\n## Two claims from the first draft of this PR that turned out to be false\n\nBoth were written confidently and both were wrong. Recording them because the corrections are the useful part.\n\n**`>/dev/null 2>&1` does not detach anything.** The claim was that the redirect \"routes the command through a shell so the work runs as a grandchild that outlives the teardown\". Both CLIs *always* invoke a hook as `bash -c \"<command>\"`, and `bash -c` `exec`s a single simple command rather than forking. Nothing was ever detached; the redirect only hid the capture's output — which is exactly what made the timeout bug take a full debugging session to find. The suffix is left in place because removing it is a separate untested change, and flagged in the Roadmap as the first thing to try.\n\n**Qwen subagents do not produce a `parent_session` record.** The claim was that one makes a subagent chat a sub-conversation. Driving a real subagent shows it runs in-process behind an `agent` tool call: no chat file of its own, no `parent_session` anywhere, so the delegation lands as an ordinary `tool_use`/`tool_result` pair in the parent. Nothing is lost — the prompt handed to the subagent, its status, terminate reason and per-tool execution summary all ride in `toolCallResult.resultDisplay` and are preserved in `raw`, checked against the captured event — but the subagent's own turn-by-turn conversation is never written by Qwen at all, so no adapter can recover it. The mapping stays (it is in Qwen's own subtype list) but is now labelled unobserved.\n\n## Verification\n\n**204 tests, 203 pass / 1 pre-existing skip.** Both adapters were then driven by hand against live CLIs, which is where the real findings came from:\n\n| | result |\n|---|---|\n| Gemini model turns + tool calls | 5 model turns, blocks correctly ordered `thinking → text → tool_use → tool_result`, **no tool call emitted twice** (the `callsInContent` precedence branch) |\n| Gemini `/rewind` | 2 `rewind` activities naming 3 and 6 withdrawn messages, and **every withdrawn message still present as a turn** |\n| Gemini catch-up sweep | killed a session with `kill -9` so `SessionEnd` never fired; a later session's hook grew its stranded tail 9 → 10 events — the first time that path has ever run |\n| Qwen | 27 events across live sessions, **no format drift**; `@file` → `context_injection`, subagent delegation captured whole |\n| Qwen headless | `qwen -p` captured, 23 → 27 events |\n\n**The demonstration nobody planned.** During one smoke test Gemini **deleted its own session file** after a `/rewind`. The conversation is intact in the ledger — the only surviving record of it:\n\n```\nseq=3  human  what is 2+2?\nseq=5  agent  Okay, I've processed the query...\nseq=9  human  what is 10+10?\nseq=11 agent  10 + 10 = 20.\n```\n\nThat is the premise of this whole project happening by accident, to a real conversation, in the course of testing it.\n\n## Known gaps (in the Roadmap)\n\n- Gemini stamps no CLI version into its session file, so gemini-cli events have no `producer.source_version` unless a caller supplies one. Reading it off the installed binary would be wrong for a backfill.\n- Signing in to Gemini CLI now needs a key — Google refuses `oauth-personal` for individuals on this client. Server-side; the transcript format is unchanged (0.54.0's record predicates are byte-identical to 0.53.1's).\n- Most Qwen `system` subtypes are still classified by name from its own `KNOWN_RECORD_SUBTYPES`. Live sessions have now exercised five of them. Notably `/chat save <title>` does **not** emit `custom_title` — it emits `slash_command` — so that path stays unobserved.\n- `cledger log` hides machinery kinds by default, keyed on the kind rather than on whose action a record was, so a slash command a person typed is hidden by the same rule as a token-count ping. Capture is unaffected (everything is recorded, `export` stays lossless); the fix worth trying is to key the default on the actor, which `activity` now carries for exactly that reason.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Ewo7xu7kzLpH4MeEDK5LDc\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/.claude/jobs/8f1a9339/tmp/prbody.md","originalFile":"Adds capture for the two CLIs at the top of the README's TODO adapter list. **Rebased onto 0.19.0** and rewritten to its bar — no skip lists, every record gets a kind, sub-conversations linked.\n\nBoth CLIs were installed and driven against a local OpenAI-compatible endpoint to produce real sessions; every format claim below comes from a live transcript or from the CLI's own shipped source, not inference.\n\n## Gemini CLI\n\n`~/.gemini/tmp/<project>/chats/session-*.jsonl`, hooked on `AfterAgent` + `SessionEnd`.\n\nThe file is append-only but is **not** a transcript — it is a write-ahead log of mutations to one conversation document: `$rewindTo` removals, `$set` metadata patches, whole-document `$set.messages` snapshots, and message upserts keyed by a durable id. Capture replays it with Gemini's own record precedence.\n\n**It deliberately does not honour the removals.** On a real session, Gemini rewrote the document with a snapshot that silently dropped the prompt the user had just typed (after a failed API call), so a faithful replay records no trace of it having been asked. The ledger keeps the union of every message the file ever held, each at its last-written value.\n\nWith 0.19.0's `activity` kind available, the rewind is now recorded *itself*, naming the messages it withdrew — so a withdrawn turn is legible as withdrawn rather than merely present. That closes the gap the first version of this PR could only list as a roadmap item.\n\nEverything else that is not a turn is recorded too: `$set` patches become `session_state`, and `info`/`error`/`warning` notices become turns spoken by the system (Gemini discards them; \"quota exceeded\" is often the only explanation for why a turn looks abandoned). Sub-sessions filed under `<chats>/<parent session id>/` are captured as sub-conversations with `conversation.parent` set.\n\nBecause nothing is ever removed, `seq` is assigned at first sighting — over messages and mutation records alike, so they share one number space and cannot collide — and never reused. A rewind therefore cannot renumber later turns; the positional-`seq` churn the opencode adapter documents and accepts does not arise here.\n\n## Qwen Code\n\n`~/.qwen/projects/<escaped-cwd>/chats/<session-id>.jsonl`, hooked on `Stop` + `SessionEnd`.\n\nA fork of Gemini CLI that did *not* inherit its conversation store: plain append-only JSONL with a claude-code-shaped envelope, so this adapter is that adapter's twin — per-line conversion, line-index `seq`, a torn-tail-safe cursor, and the same catch-up sweep for stranded transcript tails.\n\nEverything that is not a turn arrives as a `system` line discriminated by `subtype`, and each maps to a kind:\n\n| subtype | kind |\n|---|---|\n| `attribution_snapshot` | `file_snapshot` — per-file content hashes are already **in** the record, so unlike Claude Code's file history nothing needs resolving against a machine-local cache and `resolved` stays empty |\n| `custom_title`, `session_source`, `parent_session`, `goal_state` | `session_state` |\n| `at_command`, `agent_bootstrap`, `agent_launch_prompt` | `context_injection` |\n| everything else, including subtypes a future Qwen adds | `activity` |\n\nThe type list is Qwen's own `RECORD_TYPES` (`user`/`assistant`/`tool_result`/`system`), read out of its shipped sources, so only a genuinely new top-level type routes to the drift path. A `parent_session` record makes a subagent chat a sub-conversation of the session that spawned it.\n\nBoth CLIs express content as Google GenAI `Part` lists, so that conversion lives once in `adapters/genai-parts.ts`.\n\n## Shared cursor, and a bug fix for codex\n\n0.19.0 taught the claude-code cursor three things the other adapters still did not know: **absent is not zero** (a missing cursor gates the sweep), **`size` bounds the sweep**, and **the write must be atomic** (two writers). Each fix was worth having everywhere rather than in one of five copies, so the cursor moved into `adapters/common.ts` and all five adapters now use it — field names preserved per adapter, so no cursor on disk resets.\n\nThat also surfaced that **codex still had the torn-trailing-line bug** 0.19.0 fixed for claude-code: the cursor advanced past a half-written line, so the completed line was never captured. Fixed with the same narrow rule (unterminated file *and* final line) — holding at any parse failure would stall a session's capture forever on real corruption.\n\n## One ugly-but-necessary detail\n\n`install` appends `>/dev/null 2>&1` to the hook command for these two CLIs only. Verified on Qwen Code 0.21.5: `qwen -p \"...\"` fires its Stop hook and immediately exits, killing the hook process; a deliberately slow probe hook reproduced it every time. The redirection fixes it reliably, at the cost of the capture no longer printing drift warnings where you can see them — the same trade the opencode plugin already makes. Documented in the Roadmap; the real fix belongs upstream.\n\n## Verification\n\n- 197 tests, 196 pass / 1 pre-existing skip. 16 new (6 qwen, 10 gemini).\n- Real Qwen sessions captured: **46 turns, 52 activity, 20 file_snapshot** — nothing dropped, no drift warnings.\n- End to end through the installed hook: a live `qwen` run editing a file produced 6 turns, 2 file snapshots and 5 activity records, with the machinery kinds correctly hidden from the default `log` view.\n- The real Gemini session captured both messages including the one faithful replay would have lost, plus its metadata patches as `session_state`, with correct `system` vs `human` attribution.\n\n## Known gaps (in the Roadmap)\n\n- Gemini stamps no CLI version into its session file, so gemini-cli events have no `producer.source_version` unless a caller supplies one. Reading it off the installed binary would be wrong for a backfill.\n- Qwen `system` subtypes are classified by name from its own `KNOWN_RECORD_SUBTYPES` list; only two appear in the sessions on hand. Fields pass through verbatim either way, so the risk is a record filed under a slightly wrong kind, not lost content.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Ewo7xu7kzLpH4MeEDK5LDc\n","structuredPatch":[{"lines":[" Adds capture for the two CLIs at the top of the README's TODO adapter list. **Rebased onto 0.19.0** and rewritten to its bar — no skip 