dapter accepts does not arise here."," "," ## Qwen Code"," "," `~/.qwen/projects/<escaped-cwd>/chats/<session-id>.jsonl`, hooked on `Stop` + `SessionEnd`."," ","-A fork of Gemini CLI that did *not* inherit its conversation store: plain append-only JSONL with a claude-code-shaped envelope, so this adapter is that adapter's twin — per-line conversion, line-index `seq`, a torn-tail-safe cursor, and the same catch-up sweep for stranded transcript tails.","+A fork of Gemini CLI that did *not* inherit its conversation store: plain append-only JSONL with a claude-code-shaped envelope, so this adapter is the claude-code adapter's twin — per-line conversion, line-index `seq`, a torn-tail-safe cursor, the same catch-up sweep."," ","-Everything that is not a turn arrives as a `system` line discriminated by `subtype`, and each maps to a kind:","+Everything that is not a turn arrives as a `system` line discriminated by `subtype`:"," "," | subtype | kind |"," |---|---|","-| `attribution_snapshot` | `file_snapshot` — per-file content hashes are already **in** the record, so unlike Claude Code's file history nothing needs resolving against a machine-local cache and `resolved` stays empty |","+| `attribution_snapshot` | `file_snapshot` — per-file hashes are already **in** the record, so nothing needs resolving against a machine-local cache and `resolved` stays empty |"," | `custom_title`, `session_source`, `parent_session`, `goal_state` | `session_state` |"," | `at_command`, `agent_bootstrap`, `agent_launch_prompt` | `context_injection` |","+| `slash_command` | `activity`, **attributed to the human** — it carries the literal keystrokes, and Qwen marks it `sentToModel: false`, so it is not a turn the model saw but it is very much a person's action |"," | everything else, including subtypes a future Qwen adds | `activity` |"," ","-The type list is Qwen's own `RECORD_TYPES` (`user`/`assistant`/`tool_result`/`system`), read out of its shipped sources, so only a genuinely new top-level type routes to the drift path. A `parent_session` record makes a subagent chat a sub-conversation of the session that spawned it.","+The type list is Qwen's own `RECORD_TYPES`, read out of its shipped sources, so only a genuinely new top-level type routes to the drift path."," "," Both CLIs express content as Google GenAI `Part` lists, so that conversion lives once in `adapters/genai-parts.ts`."," "," ## Shared cursor, and a bug fix for codex"," ","-0.19.0 taught the claude-code cursor three things the other adapters still did not know: **absent is not zero** (a missing cursor gates the sweep), **`size` bounds the sweep**, and **the write must be atomic** (two writers). Each fix was worth having everywhere rather than in one of five copies, so the cursor moved into `adapters/common.ts` and all five adapters now use it — field names preserved per adapter, so no cursor on disk resets.","+0.19.0 taught the claude-code cursor three things the other adapters still did not know: **absent is not zero**, **`size` bounds the sweep**, and **the write must be atomic**. Each was worth having everywhere rather than in one of five copies, so the cursor moved into `adapters/common.ts` and all five adapters now use it — field names preserved per adapter, so no cursor on disk resets."," ","-That also surfaced that **codex still had the torn-trailing-line bug** 0.19.0 fixed for claude-code: the cursor advanced past a half-written line, so the completed line was never captured. Fixed with the same narrow rule (unterminated file *and* final line) — holding at any parse failure would stall a session's capture forever on real corruption.","+That also surfaced that **codex still had the torn-trailing-line bug** 0.19.0 fixed for claude-code: the cursor advanced past a half-written line, so the completed line was never captured. Fixed with the same narrow rule (unterminated file *and* final line)."," ","-## One ugly-but-necessary detail","+## The hook timeout, which is the bug worth reading about"," ","-`install` appends `>/dev/null 2>&1` to the hook command for these two CLIs only. Verified on Qwen Code 0.21.5: `qwen -p \"...\"` fires its Stop hook and immediately exits, killing the hook process; a deliberately slow probe hook reproduced it every time. The redirection fixes it reliably, at the cost of the capture no longer printing drift warnings where you can see them — the same trade the opencode plugin already makes. Documented in the Roadmap; the real fix belongs upstream.","+`cledger install` wrote `timeout: 120` into all three Claude-shaped configs. **Claude Code reads that field as seconds. Gemini CLI and Qwen Code copied Claude's hook config format but not its unit** — both pass the number straight to `setTimeout` (`Hook timed out after ${timeout}ms`, `DEFAULT_HOOK_TIMEOUT = 6e4`)."," ","+So on those two, every hook was SIGTERMed after **120 milliseconds**. A cold capture is ~85ms of work on top of node's startup, so it lost that race nearly every time — and whether `appendEvents` finished before the signal decided whether the events survived. The symptom was Gemini printing `Hook(s) [...] failed` on almost every turn while its events *sometimes* still landed.","+","+Diagnosed by trapping the signal in a wrapper script driven by a real session: the hook logged `caught SIGTERM` in the same second it started.","+","+`install` now writes each CLI's own unit, and **repairs an existing hook's timeout in place** — it previously saw \"a cledger hook is already here\" and returned, so nobody already installed could have fixed this by re-running it. `src/test/install.test.ts` is the first test this module has had, which is how a unit mismatch in a hardcoded literal survived.","+","+## Two claims from the first draft of this PR that turned out to be false","+","+Both were written confidently and both were wrong. Recording them because the corrections are the useful part.","+","+**`>/dev/null 2>&1` does not detach anything.** The claim was that the redirect \"routes the command through a shell so the work runs as a grandchild that outlives the teardown\". Both CLIs *always* invoke a hook as `bash -c \"<command>\"`, and `bash -c` `exec`s a single simple command rather than forking. Nothing was ever detached; the redirect only hid the capture's output — which is exactly what made the timeout bug take a full debugging session to find. The suffix is left in place because removing it is a separate untested change, and flagged in the Roadmap as the first thing to try.","+","+**Qwen subagents do not produce a `parent_session` record.** The claim was that one makes a subagent chat a sub-conversation. Driving a real subagent shows it runs in-process behind an `agent` tool call: no chat file of its own, no `parent_session` anywhere, so the delegation lands as an ordinary `tool_use`/`tool_result` pair in the parent. Nothing is lost — the prompt handed to the subagent, its status, terminate reason and per-tool execution summary all ride in `toolCallResult.resultDisplay` and are preserved in `raw`, checked against the captured event — but the subagent's own turn-by-turn conversation is never written by Qwen at all, so no adapter can recover it. The mapping stays (it is in Qwen's own subtype list) but is now labelled unobserved.","+"," ## Verification"," ","-- 197 tests, 196 pass / 1 pre-existing skip. 16 new (6 qwen, 10 gemini).","-- Real Qwen sessions captured: **46 turns, 52 activity, 20 file_snapshot** — nothing dropped, no drift warnings.","-- End to end through the installed hook: a live `qwen` run editing a file produced 6 turns, 2 file snapshots and 5 activity records, with the machinery kinds correctly hidden from the default `log` view.","-- The real Gemini session captured both messages including the one faithful replay would have lost, plus its metadata patches as `session_state`, with correct `system` vs `human` attribution.","+**204 tests, 203 pass / 1 pre-existing skip.** Both adapters were then driven by hand against live CLIs, which is where the real findings came from:"," ","+| | result |","+|---|---|","+| Gemini model turns + tool calls | 5 model turns, blocks correctly ordered `thinking → text → tool_use → tool_result`, **no tool call emitted twice** (the `callsInContent` precedence branch) |","+| Gemini `/rewind` | 2 `rewind` activities naming 3 and 6 withdrawn messages, and **every withdrawn message still present as a turn** |","+| Gemini catch-up sweep | killed a session with `kill -9` so `SessionEnd` never fired; a later session's hook grew its stranded tail 9 → 10 events — the first time that path has ever run |","+| Qwen | 27 events across live sessions, **no format drift**; `@file` → `context_injection`, subagent delegation captured whole |","+| Qwen headless | `qwen -p` captured, 23 → 27 events |","+","+**The demonstration nobody planned.** During one smoke test Gemini **deleted its own session file** after a `/rewind`. The conversation is intact in the ledger — the only surviving record of it:","+","+```","+seq=3  human  what is 2+2?","+seq=5  agent  Okay, I've processed the query...","+seq=9  human  what is 10+10?","+seq=11 agent  10 + 10 = 20.","+```","+","+That is the premise of this whole project happening by accident, to a real conversation, in the course of testing it.","+"," ## Known gaps (in the Roadmap)"," "," - Gemini stamps no CLI version into its session file, so gemini-cli events have no `producer.source_version` unless a caller supplies one. Reading it off the installed binary would be wrong for a backfill.","-- Qwen `system` subtypes are classified by name from its own `KNOWN_RECORD_SUBTYPES` list; only two appear in the sessions on hand. Fields pass through verbatim either way, so the risk is a record filed under a slightly wrong kind, not lost content.","+- Signing in to Gemini CLI now needs a key — Google refuses `oauth-personal` for individuals on this client. Server-side; the transcript format is unchanged (0.54.0's record predicates are byte-identical to 0.53.1's).","+- Most Qwen `system` subtypes are still classified by name from its own `KNOWN_RECORD_SUBTYPES`. Live sessions have now exercised five of them. Notably `/chat save <title>` does **not** emit `custom_title` — it emits `slash_command` — so that path stays unobserved.","+- `cledger log` hides machinery kinds by default, keyed on the kind rather than on whose action a record was, so a slash command a person typed is hidden by the same rule as a token-count ping. Capture is unaffected (everything is recorded, `export` stays lossless); the fix worth trying is to key the default on the actor, which `activity` now carries for exactly that reason."," "," 🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)"," "],"newLines":94,"newStart":1,"oldLines":61,"oldStart":1}],"type":"update","userModified":false},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"f6788994-e9cb-4111-a642-76ece2f88c4e","version":"2.1.227"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T10:59:23.874Z","schema":"annals/v1","stream":{"id":"claude-code:d10310cd-8144-4046-87b9-97bbb6ace18c","seq":1606}}
